<h2>The question</h2>

<p>The operator asked, on 2026-10-10, for "a single event stream and state manager for all": every daemon, every
operator page and every tool reading one picture of the world instead of each polling GitHub or re-scanning files on
its own. This topic holds the inventory, the measured evidence, the prior art and the design. The decision is card
xqux73p. The full working is in <a href="/reports/2026-10-10-event-stream-state-manager.md">the session report</a>.</p>

<h2>Measured on 2026-10-10</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Ledger shadow:</strong> 591 of 593 compares unreadable across 139 drain runs, every one a git
    <code>ENOBUFS</code> on a whole-file read. This one has its own one-line fix and is evidence of the pattern, not a
    reason to build.</li>
  <li><strong>operator-queue:</strong> its own <code>gh pr list</code> with comments overflowed a 1 MB buffer and hid
    all 25 open PRs.</li>
  <li><strong>Fixer:</strong> passes of 1514, 1771 and 2322 seconds; each PR planned four times with no cache.</li>
  <li><strong>Reviewer:</strong> 27 restarts since midnight, each followed by a 4–9 minute cold first tick.</li>
  <li><strong>Drain:</strong> PR listing median 16 s, p90 41 s, every pass.</li>
  <li><strong>WIP page:</strong> 1,479 full 24-hour session re-scans over about 3,958 transcripts.</li>
  <li><strong>Shape of the code:</strong> 68 <code>gh pr list</code> call expressions in 56 files, three PR-state
    caches, three host-load samplers, 20+ separate JSONL logs, and one cursor module that is not wired in.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What the world does</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Kubernetes informers.</strong> List at a version, watch from it, relist on <code>410 Gone</code>, keep
    one shared cache per process, enqueue only keys, reconcile against current state, resync slowly. One writer
    (the apiserver) with optimistic concurrency. The daemons here are controllers, so this is the main model.</li>
  <li><strong>Event sourcing and CQRS.</strong> The log is the truth for what we decided; projections are rebuilt
    from it; old events are upcast on read, never edited. GitHub stays the truth for GitHub facts.</li>
  <li><strong>SQLite WAL.</strong> Many readers, one writer, one host only. <code>node:sqlite</code> is a release
    candidate only from Node 25.7; this repo runs Node 22.1. A good local cache, not a cross-host authority.</li>
  <li><strong>NATS JetStream and Redis Streams.</strong> Durable consumers and watchable key-value stores built in,
    but a new always-on service that VMs and CI must reach.</li>
  <li><strong>Litestream and LiteFS.</strong> Asynchronous single-writer replication: backup, not shared
    writes.</li>
  <li><strong>Cloudflare Durable Objects.</strong> A single-threaded, strongly consistent object with SQLite
    storage and WebSocket hibernation. The existing webhook object is already the sequencer role.</li>
  <li><strong>Git as a database.</strong> Every write is a commit and a push; concurrent pushes collide.</li>
  <li><strong>Nomad, Consul, Temporal, CI runners.</strong> Long-poll with an index and jitter; agents connect
    outbound only; deterministic replay of a per-workflow history.</li>
  <li><strong>GitHub webhooks.</strong> No automatic redelivery of failed deliveries; a 3-day window to redeliver;
    a delivery id to deduplicate. A reconciling poll must stay.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What is already ruled here</h2>

<p>One ordered PR event log in the Durable Object (#5452, E1–E7); one portable event-log schema, Durable Objects as
the hosted authority and local SQLite never a second authority (#4601); judgments on the git ledger joined with
GitHub facts at read time; one drain writing <code>main</code>; schedule-only ticks over one shared view (#5767);
one resource sampler (#5712). Each covers one kind. Nothing joins them.</p>

<h2>The design the card recommends</h2>

<ol>
  <li><strong>One sequenced stream per tenant</strong> for every shared kind, reachable from any host; the store is
    picked by name and the default adapter is the existing Durable Object. Host-local kinds keep a per-host journal
    in the same envelope.</li>
  <li><strong>One informer library:</strong> list at <code>seq</code>, watch from it, relist on a gap, a durable
    cursor per consumer, a warm per-host cache, one fold per kind.</li>
  <li><strong>One versioned envelope,</strong> with the producer stamped from its credential.</li>
  <li><strong>One authority per kind;</strong> an authority moves into the stream only after a tested restore.</li>
  <li><strong>Leases stay conditional writes;</strong> the stream only observes them.</li>
  <li><strong>Webhooks plus one budgeted reconciler</strong> and a redeliver sweep; live per-PR probes before a
    write stay.</li>
  <li><strong>Stale is visible, never silent;</strong> unreadable is never empty.</li>
  <li><strong>No permanent dual read path:</strong> each consumer cuts over in shadow first.</li>
</ol>

<p>An independent Codex review reached the same defaults. A skeptic pass and a separate framing screen then
reshaped the forks: the ENOBUFS claim was withdrawn, per-type append permission and live-read exemptions were
added, and build order moved out of the forks into the slice plan.</p>
